    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d300">

                <pb facs="0384" n="370" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg370"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.48/4107</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 2251</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-03-02">March 2,
                        1944</date>.<lb/>[Received March 20.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to the Embassy’s despatch No.
                    2188 of February 18, 1944, in regard to the China Defence League (relief
                    organization headed by Madame Sun Yat-sen), I have the honor to enclose a copy
                    of airgram No. A–7, February 14, 6 p.m. from the Consulate at Kweilin<anchor
                        n="91" xml:id="d300-f1"/> regarding the disposition by Madame Sun Yat-sen of
                    relief funds for work in south China.</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Summary</hi>. Mr. Service states that he has been
                        informed in the strictest confidence by an American member of United China
                        Relief that Madame Sun has specified that one-third of a sum of
                        approximately CN$400,000 from her Kwantung Relief Committee in Chungking <pb
                            facs="0385" n="371" xml:id="pg371"/> be paid to the Chinese Communist
                        forces operating in the area north and east of Hong Kong for relief purposes
                        in the region under their control. The other two-thirds is to be divided
                        between the Canton International Medical Service Corps and the Kwangtung
                        International Relief Committee. <hi rendition="#italic">End of Summary.</hi>
                    </p>
                </quote>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>C. E. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
